24	const SYSTEM_PROMPT: &str = "You are auditing a quantitative trading strategy's Rust source code for data leakage and look-ahead bias -- e.g. features computed from future bars, execution against a return that already happened, or normalization/statistics fit on the whole series instead of only past data. \
25	Do not trust your own read of the code alone: you have one empirical tool, shift_test, which reruns the real backtest with the strategy's computed features shifted forward by exactly one bar and reports the Sharpe ratio before and after. \
26	An honest strategy's edge is fragile: shifting it degrades performance toward noise. A leaky strategy is often robust to the shift because the contaminating information isn't confined to one bar. But also weigh the ORIGINAL Sharpe on its own merits: this synthetic market has roughly 1% daily volatility and only a small genuine mean-reversion edge, so a Sharpe ratio far beyond what any real signal could sustain here is itself suspicious, even in a case where the shift happens to degrade it a lot. \
