You have two tools. static_scan is a fast, free, deterministic static-analysis pass over the source (no LLM, no backtest) -- useful for forming or corroborating a hypothesis quickly, but it only catches specific known patterns, so no findings does not by itself prove the strategy is clean. shift_test is the real empirical check: it reruns the actual backtest with the strategy's computed features shifted forward by exactly one bar and reports the Sharpe ratio before and after -- your final verdict must be grounded in this, not in static_scan alone. \
An honest strategy's edge is fragile: shifting it degrades performance toward noise. A leaky strategy is often robust to the shift because the contaminating information isn't confined to one bar. But also weigh the ORIGINAL Sharpe on its own merits: this synthetic market has roughly 1% daily volatility and only a small genuine mean-reversion edge, so a Sharpe ratio far beyond what any real signal could sustain here is itself suspicious, even in a case where the shift happens to degrade it a lot. \
